RULES

- "ref" is a label local to this response, used only so a position can name its
  employer and a contribution can name its position. Invent them freely; they
  are discarded once the drafts are written.

- EVERY position must carry an employer_ref that appears in employers, and every
  contribution a position_ref that appears in positions. A draft naming a parent
  that does not exist cannot be resolved and will be reported back as an error
  rather than quietly dropped. If the text describes work you cannot attach to a
  job, leave it out and say nothing — a human will notice the omission far more
  easily than a wrong attachment.

- Self-employment, freelance work, military service, locum work and unpaid
  placements are employers. Create one named the way the person names it.

- Categories are groupings the PERSON would recognise, not ones you import from
  another field. A nurse's might be "Clinical", "Charting Systems",
  "Certifications"; a chef's "Stations", "Cuisines", "Equipment". Use as few as
  the material supports. A category you invent for one item is noise.

- A tag is a specific nameable thing: "ACLS", "Epic", "sous vide", "Rule 26
  disclosures", "PostgreSQL". Not a quality ("hard-working"), not a
  responsibility ("patient care") — those belong in the contribution text.

- Claim a skill only where the text supports it. A skill is an assertion the
  person is making about themselves, and one you invented is one they will have
  to find and delete.

- proficiency is one of: {{.ProficiencyValues}}. Choose from what the text says
  about depth and duration, not from how impressive the work sounds.

- years_experience is a number or null. Null is correct where the text does not
  say; do not compute a figure from dates you are not sure about.

- Dates are YYYY-MM. Where the text gives only a year, use "-01" and let the
  reviewer correct it.

- Copy the person's own words for titles and organisation names. The reviewer is
  checking your work against their memory, and a paraphrase makes that harder
  for no gain.

PREFERENCES

- A preference comes ONLY from what the person says about what they want, will
  not do, or has had enough of. Never infer one from their history. Ten years in
  a field is evidence they worked there, not evidence they want to stay — and a
  preference you invented is one they will have to find and delete before it
  starts silently scoring job postings against them.

- If the text says nothing about what they want next, return an empty array.
  That is the common case for a plain resume, and it is the correct answer.

- preference_type routes the row at the part of a job posting it is about, so a
  row typed wrongly is checked against a field that can never answer it:
    domain        — the subject matter or industry. "paediatric oncology",
                    "freight logistics", "public sector".
    role_shape    — the shape of the job itself. "small team with high
                    ownership", "no on-call rotation", "direct patient contact".
    culture       — how the organisation works. "remote-first", "async and
                    low-ego", "up-or-out consulting".
    dealbreaker   — a thing whose PRESENCE is the objection. "night shifts",
                    "commission-only pay", "crypto".
    core_practice — a thing whose PROMINENCE is the objection: they can tolerate
                    it present, not central. "Python as the primary language",
                    "grant writing as the main duty".

- The dealbreaker / core_practice distinction is the one that goes wrong most
  often. Ask which sentence the person said. "I will not work anywhere that does
  X" is a dealbreaker. "I do not mind some X, but I do not want a job that is
  mostly X" is core_practice. A prominence label filed as a dealbreaker fires on
  any posting that mentions the thing at all.

- sentiment is "positive" or "negative" and follows the label as written. A
  dealbreaker is negative. Write the label as the thing itself — "night shifts",
  negative — not as its negation ("no night shifts", positive), because two
  labels that share vocabulary with their own opposite both match the same
  posting.

- weight is 1-10 and records how much they care, from how strongly they said it.
  "I would rather not" is a 3; "I have had enough of" is an 8.

- is_hard_gate is true only where they state a disqualifier — something that
  rules a job out rather than counting against it. A gate does not block
  anything; it is reported as a named finding. Most rows are false.

- aliases are the OTHER WORDINGS A JOB POSTING WOULD USE for the same thing, not
  synonyms of the person's phrasing for its own sake. This is the field that
  decides whether the preference ever matches: "inpatient nights" reaches a
  posting reading "three twelve-hour night shifts" only through
  ["night shift", "overnight rotation", "night float"]. A label about relocation
  needs ["onsite", "in-office", "hybrid"]. Give two to five where you can, and
  an empty array where the label is already the words a posting would use.

EDUCATION AND CREDENTIALS

- A credential is something granted by a body that can also withdraw it: a
  licence, a certification, a registration, a ticket. "ACLS", "RN licence",
  "ServSafe", "CDL Class A", "AWS Solutions Architect", "PE licence".

- A certification is a CREDENTIAL, not a skill, even though it is also a thing
  the person can do. Filing it as a skill is how a licensed professional's
  resume prints an empty credentials section while their licence sits in a list
  of technologies. If it has an issuer, an expiry, or a number, it is a
  credential.

- Education is formal schooling only — a degree, a diploma, an apprenticeship
  that conferred one. A course, a bootcamp, or a workshop that granted no
  qualification is neither education nor a credential; leave it out.

- Dates are optional on both. Null is correct where the text does not say; a
  credential with no stated issue date is ordinary and inventing one is worse
  than leaving it empty.

